WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.877
- Good morning believe
nation, today's message

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:05.338
is start now!

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:06.724
Over to you Jocko Willink.

00:00:06.724 --> 00:00:08.927
(rooster crows)

00:00:08.927 --> 00:00:10.924
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:10.924 --> 00:00:13.689
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:13.689 --> 00:00:17.189
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:22.354
- People want to know how
to stop the laziness and how

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:25.985
to stop the procrastination

00:00:25.985 --> 00:00:28.892
and they have some idea in

00:00:28.892 --> 00:00:30.391
their head, you know,

00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:34.048
some kind of vision of
what they want to do.

00:00:34.048 --> 00:00:36.938
But they don't know where to start,

00:00:36.938 --> 00:00:39.739
they don't know where to start it.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.212
You know, they don't know where to start.

00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:46.297
And instead they say
hey, where do I start?

00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:48.477
And when's the best time to start?

00:00:48.477 --> 00:00:51.876
And I have a very simple answer for that.

00:00:51.876 --> 00:00:54.876
Here and now!

00:00:57.913 --> 00:00:59.025
That's it.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:02.442
You want to improve?
You want to get better?

00:01:02.442 --> 00:01:06.434
You want to get on a workout
program or a clean diet?

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:10.254
You want to start a business?

00:01:10.254 --> 00:01:12.770
You want to write a book or make a movie?

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.362
Or build a house or a
computer or put together

00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:19.112
some mobile application?

00:01:20.088 --> 00:01:23.338
Where do you start?

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.944
You start right here.

00:01:26.944 --> 00:01:30.413
And when do you start?
You start right now.

00:01:30.413 --> 00:01:34.171
You initiate the action
aggressively, you go!

00:01:34.171 --> 00:01:37.913
'Cause the idea

00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:39.996
isn't going to execute itself

00:01:39.996 --> 00:01:43.427
and the book isn't going
to write itself, and the

00:01:43.427 --> 00:01:46.379
weights out in the gym they're
not going to move themselves.

00:01:46.379 --> 00:01:50.046
You have to do it and
you have to do it now.

00:01:52.123 --> 00:01:55.569
And that means you got to
stop thinking about it,

00:01:55.569 --> 00:01:59.205
and stop dreaming about it
and stop researching every

00:01:59.205 --> 00:02:02.719
aspect of it and reading all
about it, and debating the

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:04.201
pros and cons of it.

00:02:04.201 --> 00:02:05.868
Just start doing it.

00:02:09.205 --> 00:02:10.955
Take that first step!

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.673
And make it happen.

00:02:15.673 --> 00:02:16.886
Get after it!

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:21.053
And get after it here and now!

00:02:22.388 --> 00:02:24.467
- There is no perfect first step.

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:26.117
There is no perfect plan.

00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:28.542
There is no perfect time to get started,

00:02:28.542 --> 00:02:30.392
a lot of people are so held up.

00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:33.095
I'm finding what the perfect
first thing is to do,

00:02:33.095 --> 00:02:34.353
that they do nothing!

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.038
And that's the ultimate failure.

00:02:36.038 --> 00:02:38.804
The ultimate failure is doing
nothing and just sitting on

00:02:38.804 --> 00:02:41.498
this idea and never taking
any kind of action for it

00:02:41.498 --> 00:02:43.450
because you're worried
about making a misstep.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.064
You're worried about making a mistake,

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.507
you're worried that it's not perfect.

00:02:46.507 --> 00:02:50.201
Your perfectionism is killing
you, is preventing you

00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:53.139
from doing amazing things in
your life and with a business.

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:56.702
You take any step, you have
a vision, here's what I

00:02:56.702 --> 00:02:58.568
want to accomplish, here's
my goal, I want to write a

00:02:58.568 --> 00:03:01.058
book, I want to start a
company, whatever it is,

00:03:01.058 --> 00:03:04.690
whatever first step comes
to mind, go out and do that.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.959
And then adjust, adjust, adjust, adjust.

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:12.051
You can have the best plan
in the world, right, and I'm

00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:13.569
all for business planning.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:15.976
It's not just I'm going to
throw an idea out there and

00:03:15.976 --> 00:03:16.958
see what happens.

00:03:16.958 --> 00:03:19.372
Right, you can plan,
you can model success,

00:03:19.372 --> 00:03:21.305
you can look at what
has worked for people.

00:03:21.305 --> 00:03:23.084
But you have to go out and do it.

00:03:23.084 --> 00:03:25.769
'Cause the best plan is
obsolete as soon as you

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:27.520
start executing on it.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.880
This is what almost
killed my first business.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.655
This is how I learned the lesson,

00:03:31.655 --> 00:03:34.260
because I was that guy,
I was the perfectionist,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.475
I didn't want to do anything
till I had it all figured out.

00:03:36.475 --> 00:03:38.704
And you only later
realize, what you think you

00:03:38.704 --> 00:03:41.280
had figured out, you have no
idea what you're talking about

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.863
because you haven't gone
out and done anything yet.

00:03:42.863 --> 00:03:45.777
I lost a 40 million dollar
deal in my first company.

00:03:45.777 --> 00:03:49.725
I was making three hundred
dollars a month sucking in

00:03:49.725 --> 00:03:52.066
my company, turned it around.

00:03:52.066 --> 00:03:54.927
Started making an income
and had an opportunity

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:58.068
to sell my business for 40 million dollars

00:03:58.068 --> 00:04:00.906
and I lost it to my biggest competitor,

00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:03.636
Because I had to have the perfect plan.

00:04:03.636 --> 00:04:06.672
'Cause I spent an entire
summer working on the

00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:09.734
perfect plan and I was
too late to the table.

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.771
I was looking the wrong way.

00:04:11.771 --> 00:04:15.368
What made great sense on
paper, wasn't actually

00:04:15.368 --> 00:04:17.756
what was going to happen in the market.

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:20.172
And I lost a 40 million
dollar deal because of it.

00:04:20.172 --> 00:04:22.723
That kills, that sucks.

00:04:22.723 --> 00:04:24.887
I was 22 years old.

00:04:24.887 --> 00:04:28.410
It's important to plan,
but you need to do.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.452
And don't get so hung up
on having the perfect step,

00:04:32.452 --> 00:04:35.877
don't worry what the first step is.

00:04:35.877 --> 00:04:38.252
People write in and say what's
my first step, it doesn't

00:04:38.252 --> 00:04:39.163
matter, something.

00:04:39.163 --> 00:04:41.531
Do something and then do
something again tomorrow

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:43.218
and do something again the next day.

00:04:43.218 --> 00:04:45.352
And every day you get a little bit better.

00:04:45.352 --> 00:04:47.041
Start taking massive action.

00:04:47.041 --> 00:04:50.572
That's how you're going to get
that idea out of your head,

00:04:50.572 --> 00:04:53.647
finally, and out into the
world so you can start

00:04:53.647 --> 00:04:57.333
reaping the benefits and having
a big impact on this world

00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:00.210
like you were meant to do.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.168
So the question today is I'm curious,

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:04.048
What are you afraid to start now,

00:05:04.048 --> 00:05:07.279
that you are going to as a
result of watching this video?

00:05:07.279 --> 00:05:10.019
Leave it down in the
comments below, I'm really

00:05:10.019 --> 00:05:11.289
curious to find out.

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:13.398
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:05:13.398 --> 00:05:15.414
to Onyema Iriaka, that
you so much for picking up

00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:16.532
a copy of my book.

00:05:16.532 --> 00:05:19.073
Your one word really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:05:19.073 --> 00:05:20.743
And I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:05:20.743 --> 00:05:22.659
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:05:22.659 --> 00:05:24.313
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:05:24.313 --> 00:05:27.045
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is,

00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:28.532
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:05:28.532 --> 00:05:30.863
for another shot of N Spresso.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.702
♫ I wake up every morning

00:05:33.702 --> 00:05:36.810
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.693
(typewriter keys click)

00:05:40.693 --> 00:05:41.870
- I love when people--

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.183
I love, my assistant's got
a great thing now on this,

00:05:45.183 --> 00:05:47.830
once in every four months
someone sends an email saying,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.004
I've got a huge startup
I want you to invest in

00:05:51.004 --> 00:05:53.346
but you've got to sign this NDA.

00:05:53.346 --> 00:05:56.353
Right, which literally every
time gets an email back that

00:05:56.353 --> 00:05:57.572
says you.

00:05:57.572 --> 00:05:58.982
Right.

00:05:58.982 --> 00:06:01.592
And the reason is, I'm a
humongous believer that

00:06:01.592 --> 00:06:04.006
ideas are shit and that
execution's the game.

00:06:04.006 --> 00:06:05.981
Right. We've all got ideas.

00:06:05.981 --> 00:06:08.168
Everybody's got ideas,
do you know how many

00:06:08.168 --> 00:06:09.742
ideas we all have here.

00:06:09.742 --> 00:06:12.160
We can probably sit here
for the next two hours,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.986
draw them all out, record
them and predict the next

00:06:14.986 --> 00:06:17.786
seventy eight great startups
over the next nine years.

00:06:17.786 --> 00:06:21.854
And so I think the thing
that is another theme

00:06:21.854 --> 00:06:25.947
in entrepreneurship is
there is way too much fodder

00:06:25.947 --> 00:06:28.339
brought to the idea.

00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:31.220
Uber was Magic Cab three years earlier.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.939
Uber is not an idea.

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:34.976
Uber existed, it was called Magic Cab.

00:06:34.976 --> 00:06:38.168
But the guys that executed it, sucked.

00:06:38.168 --> 00:06:39.502
So they lost.

00:06:39.502 --> 00:06:41.004
So I think, you know,

00:06:41.004 --> 00:06:44.276
if there's any level of
romance left in this room

00:06:44.276 --> 00:06:47.030
about your idea, I'd like to suffocate it.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.442
Because I think the
actual situation is what

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:51.175
you actually do with it.

00:06:51.175 --> 00:06:53.366
- As a musician you want to be great,

00:06:53.366 --> 00:06:55.459
like you wannna be good.

00:06:55.459 --> 00:06:58.134
And you sort of set
these funny expectations

00:06:58.134 --> 00:06:59.976
for yourself you know.

00:06:59.976 --> 00:07:03.153
One of the funniest things
that Fogarty ever said to me

00:07:03.153 --> 00:07:06.495
he was like, "Yeah, I tried
to learn, I was learning to

00:07:06.495 --> 00:07:10.336
play the drums, but I realized
that I would never be as good

00:07:10.336 --> 00:07:13.628
as John Bonham from Led
Zeppelin so I stopped.

00:07:13.628 --> 00:07:16.497
And I'm like, who is
as good as John Bonham?

00:07:16.497 --> 00:07:17.330
Nobody.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.899
I mean, what the fuck,
how could you do that?

00:07:19.899 --> 00:07:23.863
So, like there should be no
right or wrong, you know.

00:07:23.863 --> 00:07:28.043
You should just be cool with
what you do and how you sound.

00:07:28.043 --> 00:07:32.216
Like, it's one of the things,
I have a problem with, like,

00:07:32.216 --> 00:07:34.962
a lot of the song contest shows on TV.

00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:38.964
Not only because, you know,
whatever it's corporate cheese.

00:07:38.964 --> 00:07:40.552
Or whatever the fuck.

00:07:40.552 --> 00:07:44.418
But I have a problem with
like if my kid walked up

00:07:44.418 --> 00:07:48.445
on stage and sang her heart
out as best as she could

00:07:48.445 --> 00:07:50.642
and Jennifer Lopez said like,

00:07:50.642 --> 00:07:52.994
"No, sorry, that's not good enough."

00:07:52.994 --> 00:07:56.188
I'd be like.
(crowd yells)

00:07:56.188 --> 00:07:57.137
Ah, Ah, Ah

00:07:57.137 --> 00:08:01.293
I'd be so pissed you know,
'cause that's not how it works.

00:08:01.293 --> 00:08:05.405
You know, you should go to a yard sale

00:08:05.405 --> 00:08:08.268
and buy a shi old

00:08:08.268 --> 00:08:12.222
guitar and then go find a
friend and get into your garage

00:08:12.222 --> 00:08:15.049
and just suck.

00:08:15.049 --> 00:08:19.067
They'd be awful and then write
some songs and become the

00:08:19.067 --> 00:08:21.947
biggest band in the world, you know

00:08:21.947 --> 00:08:26.051
'Cause that's how it works.

00:08:26.051 --> 00:08:30.174
I mean it works other ways too I'm sure,

00:08:30.174 --> 00:08:33.097
but I mean

00:08:33.097 --> 00:08:35.743
as a young musician, first of all

00:08:35.743 --> 00:08:39.025
you're not going to
make a living doing it.

00:08:39.025 --> 00:08:41.694
Like, accept that, realize that.

00:08:41.694 --> 00:08:44.651
Because then if you do, you're stoked!

00:08:44.651 --> 00:08:48.794
The reward of playing music,
playing music should be

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:52.853
the reward of playing music,
that's what you kind of get.

00:08:52.853 --> 00:08:57.010
Like when I was a little
kid, right I was playing punk

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.221
rock music as a teenager.

00:08:59.221 --> 00:09:03.085
There was never any career
opportunity cause we played

00:09:03.085 --> 00:09:05.601
punk rock music, nobody
wanted to buy that.

00:09:05.601 --> 00:09:08.680
But we loved to do it, it was so much fun.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.936
And, like if you start
from a place like that.

00:09:11.936 --> 00:09:14.840
That you just love to
create, that you just love to

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:17.125
play music and you can't
stop thinking about it

00:09:17.125 --> 00:09:19.572
and you do it every day and
you do it by yourself or

00:09:19.572 --> 00:09:21.790
you do it with your
friends and that's enough

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.298
for you to survive the rest
of your life. (audience yells)

00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:26.280
That's great!

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.302
Then once you start getting
good, you start playing for

00:09:29.302 --> 00:09:31.652
people and once you
start playing for people,

00:09:31.652 --> 00:09:34.225
if you're good, then those
people will tell other people,

00:09:34.225 --> 00:09:36.043
that girl's really good.

00:09:36.043 --> 00:09:38.141
So then all of a sudden
you're playing to all these

00:09:38.141 --> 00:09:42.201
different people and that to
me is the most important thing.

00:09:42.201 --> 00:09:45.670
When it's you on stage
and people are seeing.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.252
When people see another
person doing that it's

00:09:48.252 --> 00:09:52.151
really inspiring, like it's
cool to see a human being

00:09:52.151 --> 00:09:53.984
jamming, that's great!

00:09:55.128 --> 00:09:59.321
- I was a very headstrong young person

00:09:59.321 --> 00:10:02.911
and by 1983 or four,

00:10:02.911 --> 00:10:05.997
had started my own publishing company

00:10:05.997 --> 00:10:09.141
to publish my own books,
realizing that no one else

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:11.703
will publish me and I
was right about that.

00:10:11.703 --> 00:10:15.675
And so my first book, I saved
my food money from Black

00:10:15.675 --> 00:10:18.693
Flag, a little here, a little there.

00:10:18.693 --> 00:10:21.842
And I saved enough to make
a fold and staple book.

00:10:21.842 --> 00:10:25.023
I couldn't type so I
hand wrote out the things

00:10:25.023 --> 00:10:28.027
and I made a dummy that
I could off set print,

00:10:28.027 --> 00:10:30.137
fold and staple.

00:10:30.137 --> 00:10:33.657
And I sold them for two
dollars, mainly gave them away,

00:10:33.657 --> 00:10:36.710
but I sold enough and they
blew out, 500 just went.

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:40.075
And then I took that money to
make another print run of that

00:10:40.075 --> 00:10:43.651
and from that money I made
my first paperback and

00:10:43.651 --> 00:10:47.198
33 years later,

00:10:47.198 --> 00:10:50.083
that's a fully staffed company with

00:10:50.083 --> 00:10:53.760
books in translation
and hard cover editions

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.181
and the whole nine yards.

00:10:56.181 --> 00:11:00.040
But it started with I'm
going to do my thing, I'll be

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:02.679
City Lights junior without the talent

00:11:02.679 --> 00:11:06.294
and I did that at the
same time I was doing

00:11:06.294 --> 00:11:08.428
my first spoken word gigs.

00:11:08.428 --> 00:11:12.591
And why did I have the
gumption and audacity to start

00:11:12.591 --> 00:11:14.453
my own book company?

00:11:14.453 --> 00:11:16.793
Because I come from punk rock.

00:11:16.793 --> 00:11:19.890
I come from watching Ian
MacKaye invent Dischord Records

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.990
on his Mom's kitchen table.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:26.091
I come from folding and gluing
picture sleeves together.

00:11:26.091 --> 00:11:29.939
Black Flag had their own label
of course you do it yourself.

00:11:29.939 --> 00:11:32.634
You want to make a book?

00:11:32.634 --> 00:11:35.711
Sit down and write it then
go to the printing place.

00:11:35.711 --> 00:11:36.977
Where's a printer?

00:11:36.977 --> 00:11:38.877
Get in the phone book and find a printer.

00:11:38.877 --> 00:11:40.851
We make records, make some books.

00:11:40.851 --> 00:11:41.971
You just do it.

00:11:41.971 --> 00:11:43.889
It's like you don't even think about it,

00:11:43.889 --> 00:11:44.979
you just do it.

00:11:44.979 --> 00:11:47.721
I'm sure that that writing
is just awful, but I did it.

00:11:47.721 --> 00:11:50.501
And without even hesitation
I'm making my own book

00:11:50.501 --> 00:11:51.779
company, stop me.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:54.761
I dare you, I never
thought about it twice.

00:11:54.761 --> 00:11:58.928
Just because DIY man, like
let your voice be heard.

